---
const legend = [
  { mark: 'circle', term: 'Circles', text: 'the handle you drag' },
  { mark: 'cross', term: 'Crosses', text: 'where the constraint put it' },
  { mark: 'list', term: 'Sidebar', text: 'the parts a marker is made of' },
] as const;
---

<div class="wrapper">
  <div class="editor-page">
    <section class="panel">
      <header>
        <span class="eyebrow">React</span>
        <strong>Dragging outside the render loop</strong>
      </header>

      <p>
        The whole interactive map is a React app, so the editor started as a regular component.
        That held until I dragged a control point: every <code>pointermove</code> re-rendered a
        large subtree (solve constraints, preview SVG, update indicators, update sidebar) and the
        frame rate fell apart.
      </p>

      <p>On drag, the handlers now mutate <code>MarkerPart.controlPoints</code> directly, then:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Circles and crosses move by CSS <code>translate</code> through DOM refs.</li>
        <li>
          Each editor step is a class with its own <code>imperativeReactComponent</code>, a
          <code>createRoot</code> plus an <code>.update()</code> method, so only the part that
          changed is redrawn.
        </li>
        <li>
          Widgets that still need React (colours, text, icons) read an external
          <code>reactiveState</code> through <code>useSyncExternalStore</code>, so React re-renders
          only once that store is notified.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <ul class="legend">
        {legend.map((entry) => (
          <li>
            <span class:list={['mark', entry.mark]} aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span><strong>{entry.term}</strong>: {entry.text}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    {/* inert: real MarkerEditor chrome, playing itself back, never a control. */}
    <div
      class="editor-layer"
      inert
      data-editor-layer-island
      data-boot-module="/src/components/MarkerEditorDemo/layers/EditorLayer.boot.ts"
    ></div>
  </div>
</div>

{/*
  No <script> here — a component reached only through Stack.astro's per-layer
  Astro.slots.render() call never gets its own hoisted script onto the page (see the
  "dev-server-drops-demo-scripts" memory). EditorLayer.boot.ts is armed instead by
  Stack.astro's own script, which the pagination doesn't touch, via data-boot-module.
*/}

<style lang="scss">
.wrapper {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 100%;
  min-width: 0;
  position: relative;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
}

.editor-page {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  width: 100%;
  min-width: 0;
  display: grid;
  /* The chrome's type is bound by its own width, so the wider half is the editor's. */
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.2fr);
  gap: 0.9em;
  align-items: center;

  font-family: var(--font-poppins), system-ui, sans-serif;
  font-size: clamp(0.62rem, 0.026 * min(var(--sheet-inline), var(--sheet-block)), 1.05rem);
  color: #f4f0ea;

  /* The chrome needs 32em by 60em even in its stacked layout, which is more of a phone
     sheet than the text leaves. So portrait keeps the text and drops the editor; the
     wide sheets are where it is worth looking at anyway. Keyed on the stack's
     attribute rather than a container query: see Stack.astro's script. */
  :global([data-sheet-orientation='portrait']) & {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0.5em;
    font-size: clamp(0.8rem, 0.04 * min(var(--sheet-inline), var(--sheet-block)), 1.15rem);
  }

  /* A wide sheet has the room, and the coefficient tuned for a phone leaves the
     type at the size its neighbours only use when they run out of paper. */
  @container technical-drawing-width (min-width: 56em) {
    font-size: clamp(0.85rem, 0.028 * min(var(--sheet-inline), var(--sheet-block)), 1.25rem);
  }
}

.panel {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  padding: 0.85em;
  background: #333;
  border: 2px solid #666;
  border-radius: 0.55em;
  display: grid;
  gap: 0.55em;
  align-content: start;
  box-shadow: 0 0.35rem 0.9rem color-mix(in oklab, #000 28%, transparent);
  line-height: 1.4;
}

header {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 0.15em;

  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.7em;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }
}

p {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 0.78em;
  opacity: 0.85;
}

ol {
  margin: 0;
  padding-left: 1.15em;
  display: grid;
  gap: 0.3em;
  font-size: 0.78em;
  opacity: 0.85;
}

code {
  font-size: 0.95em;
  color: #cfe08a;
}

.legend {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0.5em 0 0;
  list-style: none;
  display: grid;
  gap: 0.25em;
  font-size: 0.72em;
  border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, #f4f0ea 22%, transparent);

  li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.45em;
  }

}

.mark {
  flex: none;
  width: 0.75em;
  height: 0.75em;
  display: inline-block;

  &.circle {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    border: 0.125em solid #6aa9d8;
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  &.cross {
    background:
      linear-gradient(#7fbf5a 0 0) center / 100% 0.125em no-repeat,
      linear-gradient(#7fbf5a 0 0) center / 0.125em 100% no-repeat;
  }

  &.list {
    background:
      linear-gradient(#f4f0ea 0 0) top / 100% 0.125em no-repeat,
      linear-gradient(#f4f0ea 0 0) center / 100% 0.125em no-repeat,
      linear-gradient(#f4f0ea 0 0) bottom / 100% 0.125em no-repeat;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }
}

.editor-layer {
  // The embed is absolutely positioned inside this box, so the box carries the
  // artwork's size or it collapses to nothing.
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 1.22 / 1;
  max-height: 74cqh;
  justify-self: center;

  :global([data-sheet-orientation='portrait']) & {
    display: none;
  }

  position: relative;
  min-width: 0;
  min-height: 0;
  box-sizing: border-box;

  :global(.editor-layer-host) {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    min-width: 0;
    min-height: 0;
  }

  :global(#marker-editor-embed.marker-editor--embed) {
    inset: 0;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
  }
}
</style>
